    "notes": ["Header order: stm32-base.org's Header 1/Header 2 tables, placed using its top-view photo, turned so USB is at the top (STM32-BASE)","Pin functions are from ST's own pin data file for STM32F103C8; it lists every possible function and does not say which need remapping (ST-PINDATA)","5 V tolerance (STM32F103x8/xB datasheet, pin table, I/O level 'FT'): PA8-PA12, PA15, PB3, PB4 and PB6-PB15 are 5 V tolerant, but only as digital inputs (not in analog mode) with the internal pull-up and pull-down off. PA0-PA7, PB0, PB1, PB5 and PC13-PC15 are NOT 5 V tolerant (absolute maximum 4.0 V); VBAT is 1.8-3.6 V. Treat the board as 3.3 V logic and use a level shifter for 5 V signals when in doubt (ST-DS)","The 5V pins connect straight to USB 5 V with no protection: never power from USB and an external supply at the same time (STM32-BASE)","The USB D+ pull-up may be 10k or 4.7k instead of 1.5k, which can stop USB from working; swapping the resistor fixes it (STM32-BASE)","User LED on PC13, lit when PC13 is low; a second LED shows power (STM32-BASE)","Regulator on stm32-base's sample board: TX6211B, 3.3 V at 300 mA (STM32-BASE)","BOOT0 and BOOT1 (PB2) jumpers; 4-pin SWD header at the end away from USB, carrying 3V3, DIO (PA13), CLK (PA14) and GND: read their order off your board’s printing (STM32-BASE)","PA15, PB3 and PB4 are also the JTAG pins JTDI, JTDO and NJTRST (ST-PINDATA)","Many boards carry fakes or clones. A genuine chip reads 'STM32F103C8'; any other marking probably means a fake, a clone or a smaller part. Remarked fakes that look genuine but misbehave have also been found (GW-FAKES)","Clones seen on Blue Pills: CS32F103 (works, but its debug ID 0x2ba01477 upsets OpenOCD until the expected CPUTAPID is changed), WCH CH32F103 (same registers, same OpenOCD fix), GigaDevice GD32F103 (a faster compatible chip) (HACKADAY-CLONES)"],
